WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.659
In the last two topics, we saw the ping command used to test connectivity to a destination and shows statistics such as percentage of dropped packets.

00:08.659 --> 00:18.360
And, we used tracert to see the path from a source to a destination and the time it takes to get data to each hop on a path.

00:18.360 --> 00:26.544
But, what if we want to see both? That is where pathping comes in. Pathping is basically a combination of ping and tracert.

00:26.544 --> 00:36.443
Pathping takes several minutes to complete, so I have already run it in this window using the pathping www.learnkey.com command.

00:36.443 --> 00:49.095
Notice it shows each hop on the path from this source to that destination, and it shows the percentage of dropped packets along the way.

00:49.095 --> 00:57.784
If you see a high percentage of dropped packets anywhere, it's probably a strong indicator of where you might be having connectivity issues.

00:57.784 --> 01:05.658
One thing to note: routers make very quick decisions in their routing tables on where to send packets.

01:05.658 --> 01:10.772
If a router cannot decide where to send a packet, it simply drops that packet.

01:10.772 --> 01:19.693
Thus, a high percentage of dropped packets could indicate a routing table that needs updating and/or rebuilding.

